From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000003 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tlb-invalidation-lab <lab@example.com>
Date: Mon, 27 Apr 2026 10:02:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] proc: document experimental per-process tlb stats sketch

This patch is intentionally a sketch, not a drop-in implementation.
Per-process TLB invalidation counters are invasive because they raise
questions about counter lifetime, attribution semantics, and `mm_struct`
size and maintenance cost. The preferred path in this lab remains
userspace aggregation from tracepoints.

The procfs shape below is therefore documented as an RFC-style ABI note
rather than presented as production-ready kernel code.
---
 Documentation/ABI/testing/procfs-tlb-stats | 31 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 31 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 Documentation/ABI/testing/procfs-tlb-stats

diff --git a/Documentation/ABI/testing/procfs-tlb-stats b/Documentation/ABI/testing/procfs-tlb-stats
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..eeeeeeeeeeee
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/ABI/testing/procfs-tlb-stats
@@ -0,0 +1,31 @@
+What:		/proc/<pid>/tlb_stats
+Date:		April 2026
+KernelVersion:	6.6
+Contact:	tlb-invalidation-lab
+Description:
+		RFC-style sketch for a per-process procfs view of TLB-related
+		observability. This file is intentionally documented as a lab
+		design note rather than a production-ready ABI.
+
+		A future implementation, if one is pursued, would need to define:
+
+		- what exactly is being counted
+		- whether counts are task-local, mm-local, or both
+		- how notifier-attributed invalidation is represented
+		- how counter lifetime interacts with exec, fork, and exit
+		- what overhead is acceptable in hot MM paths
+
+		An example output shape might look like:
+
+		experimental: 1
+		source: userspace_aggregation_preferred
+		mm_tlb_invalidations: <u64>
+		mm_tlb_bytes_invalidated: <u64>
+		mm_tlb_remote_fanout: <u64>
+
+		This repository does not claim that such an interface is
+		upstream-ready. For this lab, tracepoints remain the preferred
+		measurement path.
-- 
